The fairness review of the Tom Rhys Harries draft is done. I wrote `review-subject.json` to the run folder; it passes the review schema, and every quoted passage matches the draft word for word. Nothing else was edited or published.

I opened seven of the sources: Square Mile, both Hollywood Reporter interviews, Cultured, Mr Porter, WalesOnline and Creepy Catalog. Every quote I checked matches its source. The draft is careful overall. The problems are what it leaves out and who it says said what, not invented material.

**Two major findings:**
- **The director's answer is missing.** The title and opening are built on the torn ACL he hid. But in the same Hollywood Reporter interview, director James Watkins said knowing about the injury "wouldn't have made any difference to me." The draft leaves that out, along with the fact that he had the knee surgically repaired after filming. Watkins's line both defends Harries and tests the article's claim that fear drove him.
- **The ADHD detail is credited to him.** "He lives with ADHD" is the Square Mile writer's sentence; none of Harries's quoted words name the condition. The draft calls it his own self-description in two body passages and an FAQ answer. A whole FAQ ("Does Tom Rhys Harries have ADHD?") also turns one magazine mention of a health condition into search metadata.

**Five minor findings:**
- "Doing the stunt anyway" is wrong: he disclosed the injury before the car-impact stunt, and it was reworked.
- The Benedict Cumberbatch story drops that Cumberbatch had the same doubt about the line.
- Three guesses about his motives are written as fact.
- "Self-mutilation" is a loaded description of a joke about pulling out a tooth.
- A detail about Harries phoning co-star Max Minghella is the website's own narration, but the draft presents it as something Minghella said.

I kept the typing-is-contested flag the research stage set and added no new risk flags. I also protected five passages, including the line separating his childhood account from any diagnosis and the even-handed treatment of the tooth misquote. The similarity checker's only full match, with the Paul Bettany profile, is a standard Enneagram definition, not reused argument.

